message: "If you use this instrument, please cite it as below."
title: "Counterfactual Echo Gain (CEG): Metriplectic Assisted-Echo Instrument"
authors:
  - family-names: Lietz
    given-names: Justin K.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-9028-1366"
    affiliation: "Neuroca, Inc."
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2025-11-04
repository-code: "https://github.com/justinlietz93/Prometheus_VDM"
keywords:
  - metriplectic
  - assisted echo
  - preregistration
  - gates
  - reproducibility
  - VDM
license: MIT
abstract: >
  CEG (Counterfactual Echo Gain) is a gate-certified instrument that measures
  whether a model-aware time-reversal (assisted echo) improves recovery error
  vs a model-blind baseline, under strict physics gates. The metriplectic
  split combines a conservative J-limb (Hamiltonian/symplectic) with a
  dissipative M-limb (entropy-producing), composed via Strang splitting. A
  five-gate certification system (G1–G5) ensures physical validity before the
  CEG observable is reported.
